course: dictionary
lesson: 398
title: Apparent: clear, or only seeming true
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Apparent has two meanings. The first is easy to see or understand: clear, obvious. If it became apparent that the cat had eaten the cake, everyone could see it. The second is seeming to be true, though maybe not really true.
explain: Context tells you which meaning you need. In «mysterious and without apparent end», the tunnels seem to go on forever, but the word hints they may only look endless from where you stand. Try swapping in "clear" or "seeming": does the sentence still make sense?
explain: Apparent often appears in set phrases. For no apparent reason means there is no reason you can see. Heir apparent means the person who is clearly next in line. Learn these phrases as whole chunks, not word by word.
explain: Scientists use the word the same way. The Sun seems to move across the sky each day. This is its apparent motion. We know the Earth turns, so the movement is only apparent, not real.
fact: Apparent means easy to see or understand, clear and obvious.
fact: Apparent can also mean seeming to be true, though maybe not really true.
fact: «Without apparent end» means it seems to have no end.
fact: For no apparent reason means there is no reason you can see.
fact: The Sun's apparent motion is how it looks as if it moves across the sky, because the Earth turns.
exercise: «The tunnels branch off from a central hall, mysterious and without apparent end.» In this sentence, apparent means | Very dark | Clear and obvious to everyone | *Seeming to have no end | Made of stone
exercise: The thief left the room for no apparent reason. What does this mean? | The thief had a very good reason, shouted by everyone | *There was no reason anyone could see | The room was too small | The thief was very tall
exercise: Leo said, «It became apparent that the bridge was broken.» What does apparent mean here? | Brand new and shiny | *Clear and easy to see | Hidden under the water | Very old and grey
test: Sam says the Sun moves across the sky, but the Earth is turning. The Sun's movement is what kind of movement? | Real: the Sun really travels round the Earth | *Apparent: it only looks as if it moves | Very fast, like a race car | Hidden behind the clouds
test: Heir apparent means? | A sad and quiet person | A very old king | A bird that flies high | *The person clearly next in line
exam: In «it became apparent that someone was lying», which meaning of apparent fits? | *Became clear to everyone | Became very loud | Became tired and sleepy | Became lost in the dark
